  "nameStory": "",
  "description": "The Beach Dune Cellophane Bee (Colletes speculiferus) is a solitary bee species belonging to the Plasterer Bee family, named for their habit of lining nest cells with a cellophane-like secretion. This small bee is endemic to coastal New England and is specialized for foraging on plants found in beach dune ecosystems.",
  "idTips": [
    {
      "label": "Shiny facial structure",
      "description": "Distinctive glossy or polished appearance on the face, which is the most reliable field mark",
      "isPrimary": true
    },
    {
      "label": "Small body size",
      "description": "Typically 8-10mm in length, making it one of the smaller bee species",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Dark coloration",
      "description": "Predominantly black body with sparse pale hair and metallic reflections",
      "isPrimary": false
    }
  ],
  "habitat": "This bee is found exclusively in coastal beach dune habitats of New England, where it nests in sandy soil and forages on native dune vegetation.",
  "seasonality": {
    "spring": "",
    "summer": "",
    "fall": "",
    "winter": ""
  },
  "funFact": "Colletes speculiferus females construct underground tunnels and line the brood cells with a waterproof, cellophane-like secretion from their abdominal glands, earning the family the common name 'Plasterer Bees.'",
